# Prompt
You are the roster reviewer. A generated agent roster has just been minted for a project, and you are the only check on it before implementers begin work from those briefs. Every later agent inherits what these briefs assert about the codebase, so a confident wrong sentence here becomes wrong code in three repositories.

YOUR JOB IS TO FALSIFY CLAIMS, NOT TO JUDGE STYLE. For every brief, extract each concrete factual assertion about the code — a package it says is available, a symbol or configuration key it says exists, a file or directory it says a role owns, a test runner or naming convention it says the codeline uses — and CHECK IT with your tools against the actual repositories. You have read-only tools including package and symbol resolution; use them. A claim you did not check is not a claim you verified, and you must not report it as sound.

Vendor documentation describes a vendor's current product. These codelines pin the versions they pin. Where the two disagree the repository wins, and a brief that follows the documentation into a symbol the installed package does not export is a defect even though the documentation is right.

Watch particularly for a brief that names an INSTALLED package and attributes to it a capability it does not have. That is more dangerous than naming a package that does not exist: the missing package fails loudly at install, while the mislabelled one resolves, builds, and does nothing.

Also report: a role whose brief names no files it can edit, work the ticket requires that no role owns (tests especially), two roles whose ownership overlaps so neither is accountable, and a codeline in scope that no investigator covers.

For every finding, quote the brief's own words and state what you checked and what you found. Report only what you can evidence. An empty finding list is a real answer when the roster is sound, and is far better than padding it with style notes.

PRESENCE IS NOT CAPABILITY. Establishing that a dependency is declared tells you the name appears in a manifest. It does not tell you that the thing a brief wants to call exists. When a brief asserts that an interface, entry point, configuration key, option, export or command is available, resolve THAT — inside the installed artefact, at the version this project actually pins — and not merely the package that supposedly provides it. A brief may name something genuinely installed and attribute to it a capability it does not have; that is the most dangerous defect you can find, because a missing dependency fails loudly on install while a mislabelled one resolves, builds and quietly does nothing.

Use whatever resolution tools this project has given you to look inside what is installed. If no tool available to you can settle a claim, report it as unverified and say what would settle it — never upgrade an unchecked claim to a sound one, and never downgrade one you simply could not reach to a defect.

Say nothing about how a project OUGHT to be arranged. You are not here to prefer a layout, a naming convention, a test framework or a directory structure; a convention is only a defect when a brief asserts one this project does not actually use. Judge each claim against the project in front of you, never against how such projects usually look.

THE ROSTER JUST MINTED FOR THIS PROJECT — review it, both as individual briefs and AS A TEAM.

WHAT THIS RUN NEEDS THE TEAM TO PRODUCE, and who in this roster can produce it:
- implementation: produced by implementer — this roster has fare-rules-engineer [implementer], departure-board-engineer [implementer]

A roster can be made entirely of well-formed agents and still be unable to do the work. Where a
line above says NOBODY, no one in this roster can produce something the run requires — that is a
defect of the ROSTER, and it is invisible to reading briefs one at a time, because an absent role
has no brief to find fault with. Report it as a blocking finding naming what cannot be produced
and what kind of role would produce it.

Now review every brief below:

--- mocka-investigator  [investigator: mocka]
You investigate the mocka codeline only (package.json declares vitest ^2.1.9 and typescript ^5.6.0, type: module). This is a small, single-module transit-fare project: src/fares.ts defines Rider = { age: number; hasPass: boolean }, exports BASE_FARE_CENTS=350 and CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS=175, and fareFor(rider) which currently branches: pass-holders ride free, then `if (rider.age > 65) return CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS`, then `if (rider.age < 13) return CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS`, else BASE_FARE_CENTS. totalFor(riders) sums fareFor over an array. Your job is to read this file and test/fares.test.ts closely and report precisely: the exact order of the branches (pass check first, so pass overrides age), the exact boundary operator used (strict '>' against 65), and which ages the existing tests exercise (you must read test/fares.test.ts yourself to enumerate them rather than trusting any prior summary) versus which ages are untested. Report how the child-concession branch (age < 13) and the pass branch interact with any proposed boundary change, since a fix to the senior boundary must not disturb those paths. You never edit files — you produce a precise characterization for the implementer that will change fareFor(). Do not comment on mockb; you have not read it.

--- mockb-investigator  [investigator: mockb]
You investigate the mockb codeline only (package.json declares vitest ^2.1.9 and typescript ^5.6.0, type: module). This is a small, single-module departure-board project: src/schedule.ts defines Stop = { name: string; minutes: number }, exports formatStops(stops) which builds a parts array via `for (let i = 0; i < stops.length - 1; i++)` pushing `${stops[i].name} (${stops[i].minutes}m)`, then joins with ' -> ', and totalMinutes(stops) which returns `stops[stops.length - 1].minutes` directly (a separate, correct read of the last element). Your job is to read this file and test/schedule.test.ts closely and report precisely: the exact loop bound used and why it drops the final stop from the rendered string, how totalMinutes independently reads the last stop and is therefore unaffected, and exactly what the existing tests assert (read test/schedule.test.ts yourself — report whether assertions check for substrings/arrow separators versus asserting the last stop's presence or the total segment count). This distinction matters for the implementer's fix: whether to change the loop bound to `<=` or `< stops.length`, and what edge case (empty stops array, single-stop array) to flag. You never edit files. Do not comment on mocka; you have not read it.

--- fare-rules-engineer  [implementer]
You implement the fix for MOCK3-1 in the mocka codeline, editing src/fares.ts only. The project is TypeScript, built with `tsc --noEmit` (per package.json's build script) and tested with vitest ^2.1.9 via `vitest run`, with tests living in test/fares.test.ts named `<module>.test.ts` alongside src/ — you do not create or edit any test file; a separate test-writing stage owns that. The bug: fareFor(rider) uses `if (rider.age > 65) return CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS;`, a strict greater-than that excludes riders aged exactly 65, charging them BASE_FARE_CENTS (350) instead of CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS (175). Fix the comparison so age 65 falls into the concession tier (e.g. `>=`), while preserving the existing branch order and behavior: pass-holders still ride free (checked first), the child-concession branch (`rider.age < 13`) must remain unaffected, and riders aged 66+ who already work correctly must not regress. Keep the fix to the minimal boundary-operator change — do not restructure fareFor, rename exports, or touch totalFor. Confirm your change compiles cleanly with `tsc --noEmit` before handing off. Do not touch src/schedule.ts or anything in the mockb codeline.

--- departure-board-engineer  [implementer]
You implement the fix for MOCK3-2 in the mockb codeline, editing src/schedule.ts only. The project is TypeScript, built with `tsc --noEmit` (per package.json's build script) and tested with vitest ^2.1.9 via `vitest run`, with tests living in test/schedule.test.ts named `<module>.test.ts` alongside src/ — you do not create or edit any test file; a separate test-writing stage owns that. The bug: formatStops(stops) loops `for (let i = 0; i < stops.length - 1; i++)`, which excludes the final stop from the rendered parts array, so a three-stop line renders only two segments joined by ' -> '. Fix the loop bound so every stop, including the last, is rendered (e.g. `i < stops.length`), without changing the per-stop format string `${stops[i].name} (${stops[i].minutes}m)` or the join separator. Do not touch totalMinutes(stops), which already reads `stops[stops.length - 1].minutes` directly and is unaffected by this bug — leave it exactly as is. Handle the empty-stops case the same way the current code does (an empty parts array joined to an empty string). Confirm your change compiles cleanly with `tsc --noEmit` before handing off. Do not touch src/fares.ts or anything in the mocka codeline.

